  "result": "Behind the Screens: Uncovering Bias in AI-Driven Video Interview Assessments Using Counterfactuals (https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12114v2)\nciteturn6736view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6731view5\",\"lineno\":364}); Total lines: 541\nL360: (c) There is a slight decrease in prediction performance when using only the first 10 frames of video, but performance remains high. (d) The GAN-inverted models (MTDNN-Inv and MTDNN-Visual-Inv) perform well, though slightly below their original counterparts. (e) Across all demographic groups, average prediction performance is comparable between majority and minority groups, with no large disparities observed.\nL361: To further investigate disparities in model outputs, we analyzed the distribution of interview scores across demographic groups, as shown in Table cite108†III and Figure cite109†3 .\nL362: We also present the distribution of job interview scores across protected groups in Table cite108†III , and box plots of all OCEANI dimensions in Figure cite109†3 . We focus on the interview score because it aggregates information from all OCEAN traits and represents a practical output of affective computing models in real-world hiring scenarios. From these results, we observe that mean and median interview scores tend to differ between majority and minority groups.\nL363: In particular, the protected groups—male, older individuals ($\\text{A}\\geq 40$), and African-American individuals—consistently have lower median scores than the overall dataset median, as shown in Figure cite109†3 . Table cite108†III similarly shows small but consistent disparities in mean interview scores. For example, individuals under the age of 40 have a slightly higher average interview score than those aged 40 or older.\nL364: Likewise, Asian and Caucasian individuals have similar mean scores, both slightly higher than those of the African-American group. These differences in interview score distributions suggest potential bias in the ground-truth labels themselves, which could arise from several factors, including qualitative differences in representation, rater bias, societal stereotypes, or socioeconomic disparities that affect perceived performance.\nL365: Finally, we analyze model bias using established fairness metrics. Table cite110†IV reports mutual information scores to assess the statistical independence between interview score predictions and protected attributes. Tables cite111†V and cite112†VI present disparate impact scores based on interview score–driven candidate selection, using two common approaches: top-$N$ selection and score thresholding.\nL366: To identify thresholds that reveal the most pronounced bias, we experimented with several values and selected $N=200$ and $\\tau=0.75$ for our analyses. As shown in Figure cite113†6 , these thresholds result in disparate impact values that fall significantly below 1.0, indicating strong selection bias in the original dataset labels. As selection becomes more inclusive—either by increasing $N$ or lowering $\\tau$—the disparate impact gradually approaches 1.0, reflecting more equitable outcomes.\nL367: However, since real-world hiring decisions typically involve selecting a limited number of candidates, we adopt $N=200$ and $\\tau=0.75$ as standard parameters for comparing model bias across modalities and architectures.\nL368: In all tables presenting bias results, we include scores for both the full MTDNN model and its visual-only counterpart, MTDNN-Visual, which uses only facial features and excludes scene information. This comparison allows us to evaluate how visual inputs contribute to model bias. As shown in Table cite110†IV , moving from the dataset ground truth to the MTDNN model generally leads to increased dependency between protected attributes and model predictions, as reflected by higher mutual information scores.\nL369: Although these scores remain relatively small across all groups, they indicate a measurable shift. Similarly, as shown in Table cite112†VI , disparate impact scores often decline when moving from the ground truth to the MTDNN and MTDNN-Visual models—particularly for gender—indicating greater bias in certain cases when using visual features alone.\nL370: cite114†Image: Refer to caption L371: \nL372: cite115†Image: Refer to caption L373: \nL374: Fig. 4: Distributions of predicted OCEANI dimension scores for individuals and their counterfactual counterparts. The first row shows predictions for male individuals using the full model and the changes in scores when their counterfactual videos are input. The second row shows predictions using the visual-only model (MTDNN-Visual-Inv) for the same individuals and their counterfactuals.\nL375: \nL376: cite116†Image: Refer to caption L377: cite117†Image: Refer to caption L378: \nL379: cite118†Image: Refer to caption L380: \nL381: cite119†Image: Refer to caption L382: \nL383: cite120†Image: Refer to caption L384: \nL385: cite121†Image: Refer to caption L386: \nL387: cite122†Image: Refer to caption L388: \nL389: cite123†Image: Refer to caption L390: \nL391: cite124†Image: Refer to caption L392: Fig. 5: Example video frames showing the original, GAN-inverted, and counterfactually modified images for three individuals. For each row, the first image is the original frame, the second is the GAN-inverted reconstruction, and the third is the counterfactual frame generated by manipulating a protected attribute via latent space boundary editing (first row: gender changed from male to female; second row: ethnicity changed to Caucasian; third row: age reduced for a male individual).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBehind the Screens: Uncovering Bias in AI-Driven Video Interview Assessments Using Counterfactuals (https://arxiv.org/html/2505.12114v2)\nciteturn6736view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6731view5\",\"lineno\":395}); Total lines: 541\nL369: Although these scores remain relatively small across all groups, they indicate a measurable shift. Similarly, as shown in Table cite112†VI , disparate impact scores often decline when moving from the ground truth to the MTDNN and MTDNN-Visual models—particularly for gender—indicating greater bias in certain cases when using visual features alone.\nL370: cite114†Image: Refer to caption L371: \nL372: cite115†Image: Refer to caption L373: \nL374: Fig. 4: Distributions of predicted OCEANI dimension scores for individuals and their counterfactual counterparts. The first row shows predictions for male individuals using the full model and the changes in scores when their counterfactual videos are input. The second row shows predictions using the visual-only model (MTDNN-Visual-Inv) for the same individuals and their counterfactuals.\nL375: \nL376: cite116†Image: Refer to caption L377: cite117†Image: Refer to caption L378: \nL379: cite118†Image: Refer to caption L380: \nL381: cite119†Image: Refer to caption L382: \nL383: cite120†Image: Refer to caption L384: \nL385: cite121†Image: Refer to caption L386: \nL387: cite122†Image: Refer to caption L388: \nL389: cite123†Image: Refer to caption L390: \nL391: cite124†Image: Refer to caption L392: Fig. 5: Example video frames showing the original, GAN-inverted, and counterfactually modified images for three individuals. For each row, the first image is the original frame, the second is the GAN-inverted reconstruction, and the third is the counterfactual frame generated by manipulating a protected attribute via latent space boundary editing (first row: gender changed from male to female; second row: ethnicity changed to Caucasian; third row: age reduced for a male individual).\nL393: ### V-B Experiment 2: Counterfactual Analysis\nL394: \nL395: cite125†Image: Refer to caption L396: \nL397: cite126†Image: Refer to caption L398: \nL399: Fig. 6: Disparate impact (DI) scores for the MTDNN model as a function of candidate selection criteria, using interview score as the selection variable. The left plot shows disparate impact versus top-$N$ selection; the right plot shows disparate impact versus $\\tau$ threshold selection.\nL400: In this experiment, we evaluate our reduced-frame (MTDNN-10F) and GAN-inverted (MTDNN-Inv) models. Results for (1 – MAE) prediction scores, score distributions by protected group, and bias metrics are reported in the same tables as Experiment 1. We observe a slight decrease in interview score prediction performance compared to the original MTDNN and MTDNN-Visual models: the original MTDNN achieved 91.3% prediction performance, while MTDNN-10F achieved 90.3%.\nL401: The MTDNN-Visual-10F model, which relies solely on visual frames, produced the lowest prediction performance at 89.0%, likely due to the limited feature set. The GAN-inverted models, MTDNN-Inv and MTDNN-Visual-Inv, achieved similar prediction performance across traits and demographic groups compared to their original counterparts.\nL402: This indicates that the inversion process introduces minimal distortion and does not significantly affect model predictions, making it a suitable baseline for counterfactual analysis. The predicted score distributions by protected attribute group for MTDNN-Visual-Inv are shown in Figure cite127†7 .\nL403: When compared to the original dataset distributions (Figure cite109†3 ), we observe that disparities between protected and unprotected groups persist in the model’s predictions—not only in the interview score, but also across OCEAN dimensions. For instance, protected groups consistently received lower average scores in traits such as extraversion and openness, which are positively correlated with the interview score and may negatively affect downstream selection outcomes.\nL404: cite128†Image: Refer to caption Fig. 7: Distributions of OCEANI dimension scores predicted by our MTDNN-Visual-Inv model for individuals across different genders, ethnicities, and age groups.\nL405: Each plot shows the distribution for all individuals, the protected group (“M” for male, “Af.Am.” for African-American individuals, and “$\\text{A}\\geq 40$” for individuals aged 40 and above), and the unprotected group (“F” for female, “Asi.+Cau.” for Asian and Caucasian individuals, and “$\\text{A}<40$” for individuals under 40). cite129†Image: Refer to caption Fig.\nL406: 8: Counterfactual predictions of OCEANI dimension scores for individuals in protected groups, following latent space attribute modification using the MTDNN-Visual-Inv model.\nL407: Each plot shows the predicted score distribution for all individuals, the protected group (“M” for male, “Af.Am.” for African-American individuals, and “$\\text{A}\\geq 40$” for individuals aged 40 and above), and the unprotected group (“F” for female, “Asi.+Cau.” for Asian and Caucasian individuals, and “$\\text{A}<40$” for individuals under 40).\nL408: For protected group members, the plotted scores are derived from their counterfactual videos in which the protected attribute was modified to match the corresponding unprotected group. The horizontal grey line in each plot is the median value from the corresponding protected group distribution in Figure cite127†7 .\nL409: Next, we modify videos of individuals from protected groups using our counterfactual generation approach (Section cite18†III-C1 ) and input the resulting counterfactual videos into the previously described models to examine how predictions change when protected attributes are altered. Examples of original, GAN-inverted, and counterfactually modified video frames are shown in Figure cite130†5 . Figure cite131†8 presents the distribution of OCEANI dimension scores predicted for these modified videos.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBeyond Surface-Level Similarity: Hierarchical Contamination Detectionfor Synthetic Training Data in Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17602v1)\nciteturn6736view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6734view2\",\"lineno\":131}); Total lines: 276\nL121: Performance diverges sharply on paraphrase contamination (S2). N-gram overlap fails catastrophically (F1=0.40) as expected, while embedding similarity (0.695) and LLM Decontaminator (0.81) show partial effectiveness. Our hierarchical framework achieves F1=0.875, a 6.5% improvement over the best baseline, demonstrating that combining multiple semantic signals enhances detection robustness.\nL122: The critical gap emerges on semantic-level contamination (S3) where synthetic data clusters conceptually near benchmarks without lexical or paraphrase overlap. N-gram overlap achieves only F1=0.165, detecting fewer than one in six contaminated samples. Embedding similarity improves to 0.49 but suffers from threshold selection challenges and high false positive rates. LLM Decontaminator reaches 0.55 but remains limited.\nL123: Our hierarchical framework achieves F1=0.755, representing a 20.5% absolute improvement and demonstrating the effectiveness of embedding clustering combined with distributional analysis.\nL124: On reasoning-pattern contamination (S4), existing methods provide virtually no detection capability (F1=0.21-0.31) as this contamination type has not been previously addressed. Our framework’s CoT trace analysis provides novel detection capability with F1=0.65, though with room for improvement given the complexity of reasoning pattern comparison.\nL125: ### 4.3 Cross-Benchmark Consistency\nL126: \nL127: Table cite54†2 shows performance consistency across benchmarks for semantic-level contamination detection (S3), our primary focus.\nL128: \nL129: Table 2: Cross-benchmark F1 scores for semantic-level contamination (S3). Our method consistently improves detection across diverse task types.\nL130: Method  | MMLU  | GSM8K  | HumanEval\nL131: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL132: Best Baseline  | .490  | .490  | .490\nL133: Ours  | .755  | .732  | .778\nL134: Improv.  | +26.5%  | +24.2%  | +28.8%\nL135: Our framework achieves consistent improvements of 24-29% in knowledge-based tasks (MMLU), mathematical reasoning (GSM8K), and code generation (HumanEval), demonstrating generalization in problem domains rather than overfitting to specific benchmark characteristics.\nL136: ### 4.4 Performance-Cliff Analysis\nL137: We analyze the behavior of the model on original versus paraphrased benchmark variants to validate contamination signals. In MMLU, we observe a performance drop 18% between the original (82% precision) and paraphrased versions (64%), indicating strong contamination. GSM8K and HumanEval show moderate 8% drops. Our hierarchical framework’s performance-cliff detector successfully flags all three benchmarks, while existing methods fail to utilize this behavioral signal.\nL138: This provides practitioners with an additional validation mechanism: suspicious performance cliffs can trigger deeper investigation even when direct contamination detection proves ambiguous.\nL139: ## 5 Discussion\nL140: ### 5.1 Implications for Responsible Synthetic Data\nL141: Our results reveal a critical gap in current evaluation practices: Semantic-level contamination systematically evades detection while inflating benchmark performance. This has immediate implications for responsible synthetic data deployment. Practitioners using synthetic data generators (Self-Instruct, Evol-Instruct, or commercial APIs) should implement hierarchical detection as part of their audit pipeline before using generated data for training.\nL142: The computational overhead is modest: our full framework processes 1000 samples in approximately 15 minutes on a single V100 GPU, making it practical for production deployment.\nL143: Without effective semantic contamination detection, validation pipelines provide false confidence: models may pass token-level contamination checks while harboring subtle benchmark knowledge that inflates evaluation metrics without improving real-world capability.\nL144: ### 5.2 Limitations and Future Work\nL145: Our framework has three primary limitations. First, Level 2 semantic detection relies on embedding model quality; future work should investigate ensemble approaches across multiple embedding models to improve robustness. Second, our detection of reasoning patterns (Level 4) achieves only F1=0.65, suggesting room for improvement through more sophisticated CoT analysis or integration with program synthesis techniques for code reasoning.\nL146: Third, we evaluate controlled contamination scenarios; real-world contamination may exhibit different characteristics that require domain adaptation.\nL147: The threshold selection challenge persists across levels despite hierarchical design. Future work should investigate adaptive threshold selection using validation sets with known contamination rates or ensemble approaches that combine fixed and adaptive thresholds. Additionally, our framework assumes access to model outputs; extending detection to scenarios with only black-box API access remains an open challenge.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative Analysis of Large Language Model Inference Serving Systems: A Performance Study of vLLM and HuggingFace TGI (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17593v1)\nciteturn6736view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6731view0\",\"lineno\":295}); Total lines: 573\nL251: ### 3.5 Benchmarking Protocol\nL252: \nL253: Each experiment followed this protocol:\nL254: \nL255:   1. 1.\nL256: \nL257: Warm-up phase: 100 requests to stabilize system state\nL258: \nL259:   2. 2.\nL260: \nL261: Measurement phase: 1,000 requests with metrics collection\nL262: \nL263:   3. 3.\nL264: \nL265: Cool-down period: 30 seconds between experiments\nL266: \nL267:   4. 4.\nL268: \nL269: Repetition: 3 runs per configuration, reporting median values\nL270: \nL271: We monitored GPU metrics using nvidia-smi at 1-second intervals and application-level metrics through framework APIs.\nL272: \nL274: ### 4.1 Throughput Performance\nL275: \nL276: Figure 1 shows token throughput as a function of concurrent requests across different model sizes. Key findings:\nL277: \nL278: LLaMA-2-7B: vLLM achieves peak throughput of 15,243 tokens/sec at 100 concurrent requests, compared to TGI’s 4,156 tokens/sec—a 3.67x advantage. This gap widens to 24x under extreme load (200 concurrent requests) where TGI’s throughput degrades while vLLM maintains near-peak performance.\nL279: LLaMA-2-13B: The throughput advantage narrows to 2.8x (vLLM: 8,934 tokens/sec vs TGI: 3,187 tokens/sec at optimal concurrency) as memory pressure increases and both systems become more constrained.\nL280: \nL281: LLaMA-2-70B: With tensor parallelism across 4 GPUs, vLLM maintains 2.1x higher throughput (3,245 vs 1,544 tokens/sec). The reduced gap reflects communication overhead in distributed execution, which affects both systems similarly.\nL282: Scaling Behavior: vLLM’s throughput scales more linearly with concurrency due to efficient memory management. TGI shows throughput saturation beyond 50 concurrent requests for the 7B model, indicating memory bottlenecks.\nL283: ### 4.2 Latency Analysis\nL284: \nL285: Table 1 presents latency percentiles under medium concurrency (25 concurrent users):\nL286: Table 1: Latency metrics (seconds) at 25 concurrent users\nL287:  | TTFT  | Total Latency  | TPOT\nL288: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL289: Model  | vLLM  | TGI  | vLLM  | TGI  | vLLM  | TGI\nL290: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL291: 7B - p50  | 0.24  | 0.18  | 4.82  | 5.91  | 0.019  | 0.023\nL292: 7B - p95  | 0.89  | 0.45  | 9.34  | 14.28  | 0.037  | 0.058\nL293: 7B - p99  | 1.42  | 0.71  | 14.18  | 23.47  | 0.056  | 0.094\nL294: 13B - p50  | 0.31  | 0.22  | 8.45  | 10.24  | 0.033  | 0.041\nL295: 13B - p95  | 1.12  | 0.58  | 16.89  | 24.56  | 0.067  | 0.098\nL296: 13B - p99  | 1.78  | 0.89  | 24.31  | 38.91  | 0.096  | 0.156\nL297: 70B - p50  | 0.87  | 0.64  | 31.24  | 39.87  | 0.122  | 0.157\nL298: 70B - p95  | 2.34  | 1.45  | 58.92  | 82.14  | 0.231  | 0.323\nL299: 70B - p99  | 3.67  | 2.18  | 87.45  | 127.39  | 0.343  | 0.501\nL300: Observations:\nL301: \nL302:   * •\nL303: \nL304: TGI exhibits lower TTFT at low percentiles, making it more responsive for initial user feedback\nL305: \nL306:   * •\nL307: \nL308: vLLM shows better total latency due to faster per-token generation (lower TPOT)\nL309: \nL310:   * •\nL311: \nL312: The latency gap widens at high percentiles, with vLLM showing 1.5-1.7x better p99 latencies\nL313: \nL314:   * •\nL315: \nL316: For the 70B model, both systems face significant latency challenges, but vLLM maintains better tail performance\nL317: ### 4.3 GPU Utilization and Memory Efficiency\nL318: \nL319: #### 4.3.1 GPU Compute Utilization\nL320: \nL321: Figure 2 illustrates GPU utilization across concurrency levels:\nL322: \nL323: vLLM: Achieves 85-92% GPU utilization under high concurrency, enabled by efficient continuous batching and PagedAttention’s reduced memory overhead.\nL324: \nL325: TGI: Peaks at 68-74% utilization, with memory constraints limiting batch sizes and leaving compute underutilized.\nL326: #### 4.3.2 Memory Usage\nL327: \nL328: Table 2 shows peak GPU memory consumption:\nL329: \nL330: Table 2: Peak GPU memory usage (GB) at 50 concurrent requests\nL331: Model  | vLLM  | TGI\nL332: --- | --- | ---\nL333: LLaMA-2-7B  | 24.3  | 31.7\nL334: LLaMA-2-13B  | 42.8  | 54.2\nL335: LLaMA-2-70B (per GPU)  | 68.9  | 76.4\nL336: \nL337: vLLM’s PagedAttention reduces memory consumption by 19-27% through elimination of fragmentation, enabling larger batch sizes in the same memory footprint.\nL338: ### 4.4 Scalability Characteristics\nL339: \nL340: We examined how performance scales with increasing load:\nL341: \nL342: vLLM Scaling: Throughput increases linearly up to 100-150 concurrent requests before plateauing. The system maintains stable latency until saturation, after which both latency and throughput degrade gracefully.\nL343: \nL344: TGI Scaling: Shows earlier saturation (50-75 concurrent requests) with more pronounced latency increases beyond this point. Memory constraints become the primary bottleneck.\nL345: ### 4.5 Request Completion Patterns\nL346: \nL347: Analyzing request completion times reveals interesting scheduling differences:\nL348: \nL349: vLLM: Implements a more aggressive continuous batching strategy, resulting in tighter clustering of completion times but occasionally longer tail latencies for unlucky requests.\nL350: \nL351: TGI: Shows more variance in completion times but with better fairness—requests have more predictable latency regardless of when they arrive.\nL352: \nL353: ## 5 Analysis and Discussion\nL354: ### 5.1 Architectural Factors\nL355: \nL356: The performance differences stem from fundamental architectural choices:\nL357: #### 5.1.1 Memory Management\nL358: \nL359: vLLM’s PagedAttention revolutionizes KV cache management by:\nL360: \nL361:   1. 1.\nL362: \nL363: Eliminating fragmentation: Non-contiguous memory allocation means no wasted space between variable-length sequences\nL364: \nL365:   2. 2.\nL366: \nL367: Enabling sharing: Common prompt prefixes can share KV cache blocks, reducing redundancy in scenarios like few-shot learning\nL368: \nL369:   3. 3.\nL370: \nL371: Flexible allocation: Memory blocks are allocated on-demand rather than pre-allocated for maximum length\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTensor Gauge Flow Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17616v1)\nciteturn6736view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17616v1\",\"lineno\":134}); Total lines: 466\nL102: #### 2.1.1  Tensor Fields on Fiber Bundles\nL103: \nL104: In mathematical terms, an $(m,n)$-Tensor Field $\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}$ on a manifold $M$ is a smooth section:\nL105: \nL106:  | $$\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}\\in\\bigl\\{\\,S:M\\rightarrow T^{(m,n)}M\\ \\big|\\ \\pi\\circ S=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\,\\bigr\\},$$  |\nL107: \nL108: where the $(m,n)$-Tensor bundle is\nL109: \nL110:  | $$T^{(m,n)}M:=\\bigsqcup_{x\\in M}T^{(m,n)}_{x}M,$$  |\nL111: \nL112: with projection $\\pi:T^{(m,n)}M\\rightarrow M$, and fibers\nL113: \nL114:  | $$T^{(m,n)}_{x}M:=(T_{x}M)^{\\otimes m}\\otimes(T^{*}_{x}M)^{\\otimes n}.$$  |\nL115: In local coordinates $x^{\\mu}$, a Tensor Field has the form\nL116: \nL117:  | $$\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}=\\hat{T}^{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{m}}{}_{\\nu_{1}\\dots\\nu_{n}}\\,\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^{\\mu_{1}}}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x^{\\mu_{m}}}\\otimes dx^{\\nu_{1}}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes dx^{\\nu_{n}}.$$  |\nL118: A (smooth) Fiber Bundle is a surjective map $\\pi:E\\rightarrow M$ with typical fiber $F$ such that for every $x\\in M$ there is an open neighborhood $U$ and a diffeomorphism $\\phi_{U}:\\pi^{-1}(U)\\!\\to U\\times F$ satisfying $\\mathrm{pr}_{1}\\circ\\phi_{U}=\\pi$ (local triviality).\nL119: There are now two possibilities to express a Tensor Gauge Field on a Fiber Bundle $E\\rightarrow M$:\nL120: \nL121:   1. 1.\nL122: \nL123: Tensor Field on the base manifold valued in the fiber:\nL124:  | $$\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}\\;\\in\\;\\bigl\\{\\,S:M\\rightarrow T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes E\\ \\big|\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes E}\\circ S=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\,\\bigr\\},$$  |\nL125: \nL126: valued in the typical fiber $F$ (assuming $E$ is a vector bundle, e.g., an associated bundle).\nL127: \nL128:   2. 2.\nL129: \nL130: Tensor Field on the total space:\nL131: \nL132:  | $$\\hat{T}^{(m,n)}\\;\\in\\;\\bigl\\{\\,S:E\\rightarrow T^{(m,n)}E\\ \\big|\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}E}\\circ S=\\mathrm{Id}_{E}\\,\\bigr\\}.$$  |\nL133: ## 3  Tensor Gauge Flow Models\nL134: \nL135: A Tensor Gauge Flow Model is defined on a (graded) Fiber Product Bundle $\\hat{A}$:\nL136: \nL137:  | $$\\hat{A}=B\\times_{M}C$$  |\nL138: \nL139: where $B\\to M$ and $C\\to M$ are smooth Fiber Bundles over the same base:\nL140: \nL141: The dynamics of the Tensor Gauge Flow Model is governed by the following ODE:\nL142:  | $\\displaystyle\\hat{\\nabla}_{dt}x(t)=\\;v_{\\theta}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\;-\\;\\alpha(x(t),t)\\,\\Pi_{M}\\!\\left(\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right]\\,\\prod_{i=1}^{n}d^{\\mu_{i}}\\!\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right)$  |\nL143: \nL144: with the following ingredients:\nL145: \nL146:   * •\nL147: \nL148: $v_{\\theta}(x(t),t)\\in TM$ represents a learnable vector field modeled by a Neural Network.\nL149: \nL150:   * •\nL151: \nL152: $\\alpha(x(t),t)$ is a space-and time-dependent scalar weight, also modeled by a Neural Network.\nL153: \nL154:   * •\nL155: $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)$ is the Tensor Gauge Field modeled by a Neural Network.\nL156: \nL157:   * •\nL158: \nL159: $d^{\\mu}(x(t),t)\\in TM$ is the direction vector field.\nL160: \nL161:   * •\nL162: \nL163: $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ is a Tensor Field on $C$, which can be parameterized by a Neural Network.\nL164: \nL165:   * •\nL166: \nL167: $\\Pi_{M}:C\\rightarrow TM$ is a smooth bundle map projection from the vector bundle $C$ to the tangent bundle $TM$ of the base manifold $M$.\nL168: The Tensor Gauge Field $\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)$ is encoded by the Fiber Bundle $B$. The fibers of $B$ are different for the following two cases:\nL169: \nL170:   * •\nL171: \nL172: $L_{\\infty}$-algebra-valued case: The fibers of $B$ carry a graded vector space $\\hat{B}$ underlying an L_{∞}-algebra, and $\\mathcal{A}$ is a smooth section of the tensor bundle $T^{(m,n)}M$ with values in $\\hat{B}$:\nL173:  | $$\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)\\in\\{\\mathcal{A}:M\\to T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\hat{B},\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\hat{B}}\\circ\\mathcal{A}=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\}$$  |\nL174: \nL175:   * •\nL176: \nL177: Lie-algebra-valued case: The fibers of $B$ carry a Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{g}$:\nL178: \nL179:  | $$\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}(x(t),t)\\in\\{\\mathcal{A}:M\\to T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\mathfrak{g},\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\mathfrak{g}}\\circ\\mathcal{A}=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\}$$  |\nL180: The corresponding Tensor Field $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ is a Tensor Field on $C$ with typical fiber $P$, such that:\nL181: \nL182:   * •\nL183: \nL184: $L_{\\infty}$-algebra-valued case: $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ takes values in a graded fiber $\\hat{P}$, which is equipped fibrewise with the same L_{∞}-algebra structure as $\\hat{B}$:\nL185: \nL186:  | $$\\hat{T}(x(t),t)\\in\\{\\hat{T}:M\\to T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\hat{P},\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes\\hat{P}}\\circ\\hat{T}=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\}$$  |\nL187: \nL188:   * •\nL189: Lie-algebra representation case: $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$ takes values in a representation $P$ of $\\mathfrak{g}$:\nL190: \nL191:  | $$\\hat{T}(x(t),t\\in\\{\\hat{T}:M\\to T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes P,\\ \\pi_{T^{(m,n)}M\\otimes P}\\circ\\hat{T}=\\mathrm{Id}_{M}\\}$$  |\nL192: \nL193: The action of the Tensor Gauge Field $\\mathcal{A}$ on the Tensor Field $\\hat{T}$ differs in the two cases:\nL194: \nL195:   * •\nL196: \nL197: Action of a Lie algebra $\\mathfrak{g}$ on the Tensor Field $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$:\nL198:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{A}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right]:=\\;\\mathcal{A}^{a}_{\\mu_{1}\\dots\\mu_{n}}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\,L_{a}\\!\\left[\\hat{T}\\bigl(x(t),t\\bigr)\\right],$  |\nL199: with $L_{a}$ the basis elements of $\\mathfrak{g}$ acting in the representation carried by $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$. The Tensor Field may be an ordinary vector, $\\hat{T}^{\\alpha}(x(t),t)=\\hat{v}^{\\alpha}(x(t),t)$, or a contraction $\\hat{T}^{\\alpha}(x(t),t)=\\sum_{k}T^{\\alpha}_{\\nu_{1}\\dots\\nu_{k}}\\,\\hat{v}^{\\nu_{1}}\\!\\cdots\\hat{v}^{\\nu_{k}}$.\nL200: \nL201:   * •\nL202: \nL203: Action of an $L_{\\infty}$-algebra on the (graded) Tensor Field $\\hat{T}(x(t),t)$:\n"
}
